para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">As mentioned, Manitoba continues to account for approximately 3&percnt; of Canada&apos;s total emissions, with Canada in turn accounting for approximately 1.<trace.deleted/><trace>4</trace>&percnt; of the world&apos;s total emissions.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">7</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Monica Crippa et al, &ldquo;GHG Emissions of All World Countries &ndash; 2025 Report&rdquo; (2025), online: <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://data.europa.eu/doi/10.2760/9816914</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> According to the Auditor General&apos;s 2017 Report, Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions are anticipated to grow to approximately 23 Mt by 2030. <trace>However, it is important to note that smoke from forest fires is expected to be a significant source of emissions in 2025 and may affect those numbers. Manitoba has set two Emissions Targets thus far, both of which are for cumulative GHG emissions reductions. The 2024 NIR shows that Manitoba&apos;s 2018-2022 Emissions Reductions Target of 1 Mt cumulative emissions reduction target has been achieved, with reductions totalling 2.45 Mt for this period. Manitoba&apos;s current goal is to reduce emissions by 5.6 Megatonnes (Mt) between 2023-2027.<footnote date.updated="1" date.trace="1"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">8</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Government of Manitoba &ldquo;Climate Action: Tracking Progress&rdquo; (Accessed 29 May 2026), online: <cite finder="legacy" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref">https://www.gov.mb.ca/climateaction/trackingprogress/index.html</ref></cite></para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></trace><trace.deleted/><trace>On top of that,</trace> Environment and Climate Change Canada modelled projected emissions of Manitoba (including implemented provincial and federal environmental measures within the model). The model suggests provincial emissions will remain below 23 Mt in the year 2030.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb427f05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Environment and Climate Change Canada, Canada&apos;s Greenhouse Gas and Air Pollutant Emissions Projections 2021 (2022), at pp.12 and 34-36</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb427f15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://publications.gc.ca/collections/collection_2022/eccc/En1-78-2021-eng.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para></section.body></section></section.block></analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">B</designator><name date.updated="0">Climate Change Impacts</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:4</designator><name date.updated="0">Historical Impacts</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">There is evidence that Manitoba is experiencing climate change impacts.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See, for example, International Institute for Sustainable Development, Climate Change Impacts in Manitoba (March 2007). The IISD, was established in 1990 and is a Canadian-based, not-for-profit organization that engages in policy research in pursuit of sustainable development. For more information on the IISD see &laquo;www.iisd.org&raquo; (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> There are also recent reported occurrences that may be climate change impacts: the winter road system, consisting of ice roads developed over lakes and other obstacles in order to provide seasonal access to remote, northern communities, have become less reliable; seasons have become shorter because of higher temperatures and earlier melting; polar bear populations have, at least anecdotally, been observed to be in decline as has the weight of bears; there are more icefree days in the Arctic regions; and unpredictable weather and increases in extreme weather events including insufficient or excessive rainfall and snowfall events as well as tornados. On June 22, 2007, Canada&apos;s first category F5 tornado (the most powerful category) hit the town of Elie, Manitoba 40 kilometers west of Winnipeg.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For example, in the last century, Manitoba&apos;s temperature has increased by 1.0&deg; Celsius which is higher than the 0.6&deg; Celsius global average. See Climate Change Connection, &ldquo;Breaking Records&rdquo;, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb476105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/6S2Q-8C9S</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).See also <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb476115f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">ClimateWest, Prairie Region Adaptation Initiative, Preparing for Climate Change in the Prairie Provinces: Regional survey outcomes on the climate adaptation priorities and progress of communities (December 2021)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb49d205f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://climatewest.ca/publications/preparing-for-climate-change-in-the-prairie-provinces-regional-survey-outcomes-on-the-climate-adaptation-priorities-and-progress-of-communities/</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para></section.body></section><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:5</designator><name date.updated="0">Projected Impacts</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba expects to see earlier and more significant climate changes than many other parts of the country. In 2001, the Manitoba Clean Environment Commission<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The Manitoba Clean Environment Commission is an arms-length provincial agency established under <ital>The Environment Act</ital> that &ldquo;encourages and facilitates public involvement in environmental matters and offers advice and recommendations to the Minister of Sustainable Development with respect to environmental issues, project approvals and environmental licences&rdquo;. See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb4c4305f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission at &laquo;www.cecmanitoba.ca&raquo; (accessed May 2022)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> and the International Institute for Sustainable Development released <ital>Manitoba and Climate Change: A Primer</ital>,<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb4c4315f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission and the International Institute for Sustainable Development, Manitoba Climate Change: A Primer (2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb4eb405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/V57X-LWPL</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> which describes a number of anticipated climate change impacts for Manitoba. The province expects to see significant climate change effects on long-term weather patterns, some of which would not necessarily be <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;15" nep=";;17" hyphenated="n" folio="8-15" backFolio="8-16" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>adverse to living conditions or to all economic activities in the province. Manitobans could see a longer growing season, reduced winter heating costs and an extended shipping season at the Port of Churchill, the only seaport in the prairie provinces. On the other hand, transportation to Churchill may be hindered by melting permafrost, resulting in instability in the Hudson Bay Railway line, Churchill&apos;s only transportation link to the south.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Southern Manitoba, where the vast majority of the population resides, currently experiences a northern continental climate. Northern Manitoba has a sub-arctic climate. With climate change, summers are expected to be warmer and drier while winters are expected to be warmer with greater precipitation, and in all seasons, more variability in precipitation is expected from year to year. Extreme weather events and their intensity are also expected to increase.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb4eb415f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission and the International Institute for Sustainable Development, Manitoba Climate Change: A Primer (2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb4eb425f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/V57X-LWPL</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba is expected to experience mid-season thaws in winter, resulting in increased risk of flood due to frozen conditions and ice damming. Spring flooding, already a significant concern in some parts of southern Manitoba, may increase due to variability of climatic conditions. On the other hand, summer river flows are expected to decrease due to reduced snow melt and glacial run-off from the Alberta Rockies. This will be of particular significance to northern rivers and Lake Winnipeg.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">4</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb512505f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission and the International Institute for Sustainable Development, Manitoba Climate Change: A Primer (2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb512515f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/V57X-LWPL</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Increased temperatures are expected to move the range of the boreal forest northward. This raises the question as to whether certain natural species will adapt sufficiently to the climatic changes such as changes in temperature and precipitation as well as issues related to the movement of previously foreign species northward. Periods of significantly drier weather, or even drought, also increase the risk of forest fires, insect outbreaks, invasive insect species and severe weather events that all result in destruction of forest.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">5</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb539605f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission and the International Institute for Sustainable Development, Manitoba Climate Change: A Primer (2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb539615f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/V57X-LWPL</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">With respect to potential benefits, a longer growing season could result in the ability to grow a greater variety of crops similar to those grown in the central United States. On the other hand, reduced water supplies, the potential for increased drought and the possibility of more frequent and severe weather events such as hail storms and tornados may adversely affect productivity and the ability to maintain rural population centres. Increased temperatures also bring the risk of an increased range of weeds and insect pests.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">6</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb560705f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Clean Environment Commission and the International Institute for Sustainable Development, Manitoba Climate Change: A Primer (2001)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb587805f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://perma.cc/V57X-LWPL</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para></section.body></section></section.block></analytical.level.body></analytical.level></analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">III</designator><name date.updated="0">Legal Framework</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">A</designator><name date.updated="0">Constitutional Framework</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.I.1.a Environmental law&mdash;Constitutional issues&mdash;Jurisdiction to enact environmental legislation&mdash;Constitutional authority</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:6</designator><name date.updated="0">Generally</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">The reader is referred to <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb5ae905f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secrange="y" secref="6:1 et seq.">&dblsect;6:1 et seq.</ref></cite>, &ldquo;Alberta&rdquo;, for a discussion of the constitutional framework affecting the Prairie Provinces. Like Alberta and Saskatchewan, Manitoba did not receive jurisdiction over its natural resources until the <ital>Natural Resources Transfer Agreement</ital> of 1930.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Manitoba Natural Resources Transfer Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. N30.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Today however, Manitoba has this jurisdiction, as well as other constitutional power in which to ground its regulation of climate change matters. Manitoba did not take issue with shared jurisdiction over climate change regulation with the federal government until 2017, where it was one of two provinces to withhold from signing a federal climate-change framework agreement. In August 2017, the Manitoba government sought a legal opinion <ital>inter alia</ital> on the constitutional authority of the federal government to implement a carbon pricing scheme. The opinion concluded that the federal government likely had such authority pursuant to its taxation power under s. 91(3) of the Constitution. Furthermore, the scheme&apos;s &ldquo;backstop&rdquo; feature which allows provincial legislatures to opt-out of the federal carbon pricing scheme by enacting provincial legislation to meet or exceed federal objectives was found to be consistent with the concept of &ldquo;cooperative federalism&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">2</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb5d5a05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Bryan P. Schwartz, &ldquo;Legal Opinion on the Constitutionality of the Federal Carbon Pricing Benchmark &amp; Backstop Proposals&rdquo; (October 6. 2017)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb5d5a15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/federal_carbon_pricing_benchmark_backstop_proposals.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). For further discussion on climate change and the federal versus provincial jurisdiction debate, see generally <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb5d5a25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">A.R. Lucas, &ldquo;Legal Constraints and Opportunities: Climte Change and the Law&rdquo;, in H. Coward and A.J. Weaver, eds., Hard Choices: Climate Change in Canada (Kitchener-Waterloo: Wilfred Laurier University Press, 2004)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Manitoba government accordingly moved forward with its own <ital>Made-in-<?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;17" nep=";;19" hyphenated="n" folio="8-17" backFolio="8-18" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>Manitoba Climate and Green Plan</ital>. Subsequently, and notwithstanding Manitoba continuing on with other initiatives pursuant to the Plan, Manitoba&apos;s challenge of the federal government&apos;s decision to impose its carbon tax scheme in Manitoba was decided in favour of the federal government. Manitoba chose not to appeal the decision.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">3</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bb5fcb05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba Confirms No Appeal in Carbon Tax Case&rdquo;, News Release (November 17, 2021)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bb5fcb15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;52739&amp;posted&equals;2021-11-17</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). See also <cite finder="manual" type="case"><ref p1="Manitoba" p2="Canada (Attorney General)" post.rptr="4640" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I0bb623c05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" title="Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General)">Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General), 2021 CarswellNat 4640, 2021 CarswellNat 4641, 2021 FC 1115, 44 C.E.L.R. (4th) 273, 2021 D.T.C. 5118 (F.C.)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> For further discussion see <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb623c15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:18">&sect;8:18</ref></cite> and <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bb623c25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:19">&sect;8:19</ref></cite> of this chapter.</para.text></para></section.body></section></section.block></analytical.level.body></analytical.level><analytical.level date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><designator date.updated="0">B</designator><name date.updated="0">Legislative and Regulatory Framework</name></outline.name.block></front><analytical.level.body date.updated="0"><section.block><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:7</designator><name date.updated="0">Introduction</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Manitoba has been active in the development of legislation dealing with protection of the environment and human health and safety within the confines of Manitoba&apos;s constitutional powers. This section will describe the effects of existing Manitoba legislation that is not specific to solely addressing climate change issues on GHG regulation and the province&apos;s response to climate change.</para.text></para></section.body></section><section date.revised="0" date.updated="0"><metadata.block metadata.store="yes"><metadata field="classification"><value>ENV.III.1.b Environmental law&mdash;Statutory protection of environment&mdash;Environmental assessment&mdash;Provincial assessment</value></metadata></metadata.block><section.front date.updated="0"><outline.name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><label date.updated="0">&sect;</label><designator date.updated="0">8:8</designator><name date.updated="0">Environment<sep/>General Legislation</name></outline.name.block></section.front><section.body date.updated="0"><head align="l" toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name.block toc.include="y" date.updated="0"><name date.updated="0"><ital><bold>The Environment Act</bold></ital><footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">1</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><ital>The Environment Act</ital>, C.C.S.M., c. E125.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></name></name.block></head><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">